WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.451
Monitoring scope should reflect the organization's risk and business context.

00:00:04.451 --> 00:00:09.237
Most programs need visibility into asset inventory, cloud resources, endpoints, servers,

00:00:09.237 --> 00:00:12.717
applications, identities, privileged access, network exposure, security configurations,

00:00:12.717 --> 00:00:17.068
vulnerabilities, patches, logs, alerts, data access, backup status, control evidence,

00:00:17.068 --> 00:00:18.373
and third-party dependencies.

00:00:18.373 --> 00:00:23.159
The exact list will vary, but the organizing principle is simple:

00:00:23.159 --> 00:00:25.770
monitor what can materially change risk.

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:30.991
Asset monitoring is foundational because teams cannot protect what they cannot see.

00:00:30.991 --> 00:00:34.907
Cloud resources, virtual machines, containers, databases, storage locations, endpoints,

00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:38.387
applications, and external services need ownership and context.

00:00:38.387 --> 00:00:44.043
Unknown assets often become unmanaged assets, and unmanaged assets tend to miss patching,

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:47.089
logging, configuration, backup, and access review expectations.

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:52.745
Inventory is not paperwork; it is the map that makes other monitoring possible.

00:00:52.745 --> 00:00:55.355
Identity monitoring is just as important.

00:00:55.355 --> 00:01:00.141
New accounts, stale accounts, privileged role assignments, service accounts, access tokens,

00:01:00.141 --> 00:01:02.317
key rotation, failed authentication patterns,

00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:05.797
and unusual administrative activity can all affect risk.

00:01:05.797 --> 00:01:11.018
Privileged identities deserve especially close monitoring because they can create high-impact changes.

00:01:11.018 --> 00:01:17.980
A small identity mistake can become a large system problem if the account has broad authority.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.460
Monitoring also extends to dependencies and control evidence.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.376
A third-party service may affect availability, logging, vulnerability exposure,

00:01:25.376 --> 00:01:26.681
or inherited controls.

00:01:26.681 --> 00:01:28.857
Backup status may determine recoverability.

00:01:28.857 --> 00:01:33.643
Control evidence may show whether access reviews, encryption checks, vulnerability remediation,

00:01:33.643 --> 00:01:37.558
incident response readiness, and logging coverage are actually operating.

00:01:37.558 --> 00:01:41.474
The scope should be broad enough to support decisions,

00:01:41.474 --> 00:01:46.260
but focused enough that owners can act on what is found.
